      "Opponents, including many doctors, disability rights campaigners and religious leaders, fear that vulnerable people could feel pressure to end their lives so as not to be a burden on their families or on an overstretched health service. They point to Canada, where eligibility for medical assistance in dying has been widened since its introduction in 2016, as evidence of a “slippery slope”. Others warn that palliative care in Britain is underfunded and that people should have access to good end-of-life care before being offered help to die.",
      "The bill must now be examined by the House of Lords, where many peers have expressed strong reservations and are expected to propose numerous amendments. The unelected chamber cannot normally block a bill indefinitely, but it can delay it and force changes. Its supporters hope the law could come into force within a few years; its opponents hope to stop it altogether.",
      "Whatever the outcome, the vote shows how societies are rethinking the end of life. Several countries and US states have legalised some form of assisted dying, and in France too, Parliament has been debating a bill on the subject.",
