      "The convention was drafted in 1950 by the Council of Europe, an organisation that is separate from the European Union and today has 46 member states. British lawyers played a leading role in writing it, and Winston Churchill was among the most prominent supporters of the project. The convention protects rights such as the right to life, the prohibition of torture, the right to a fair trial and freedom of expression. Individuals who believe their rights have been violated can, once they have exhausted national remedies, bring a case before the European Court of Human Rights in Strasbourg.",
